From d81773d899b2f5617c85f94748ce2c35dceae66c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HempFiend <17104973+jaidenscool1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 14:42:31 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] PROBE: EDEN_NX2_PT_CAPTURE, record one frame of guest GPU
 work for the passthrough replay

Command list entries in execution order, each channel's engine state (3D registers, native compute
registers, MME code/start addresses/data RAM, 3D constant buffer bindings), the GPU VA map, device
memory at the start (after flushing GPU-dirty mappings) and at the end, and the inline QMDs the compute
engine writes. Touch <dir>/capture.now in game: records from the next present for two presents.

Also records the PTE kinds the guest asks for (nx2:gpu 212 modify, 216 sparse map; 211 maps and 213
unmaps forget them) as kinds.txt: Eglantine maps everything as pitch, but the host GPU samples depth
texture headers only from pages of the matching depth kind. Each channel's compute MME code and start
addresses are the image its macros run (the compute one once the channel uploaded through the compute
class, else the 3D one), not always the 3D image.

Also traces 3D macros on the split front (lower-case c/e lines in mme.txt; complete with
EDEN_NX2_PERF_OFF=macro_batch,dma_forward and EDEN_NX2_INDIRECT_RECORDS=0), and records the guest bytes the
back reads per entry from native uniform banks whose pages changed (deltas.bin; every bind with
EDEN_NX2_BANK_REUPLOAD=3). While recording, each entry drains the split first, so what the back notes
belongs to the entry it executes.

Also records the guest bytes the back reads at every other read site while recording: buffer uploads (site 1),
every uniform bind not over GPU-modified memory (site 2) and texture uploads (site 3, GPU VA split over the
VA map); the site is the record entry's top 4 bits and a record equal to the last one at its address is
dropped. While a dump flushes, logs once per image the images the texture cache cannot download (their data
never reaches the dump).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
